---
layout: none
sitemap: false
---
{%- comment -%}
  The search index. One record per searchable THING, built by Liquid at build
  time — no plugin, no Node step, nothing GitHub Pages cannot run.

  A "thing" is not the same as a page. An example section is a page and is one
  record; a /in-the-wild/ entry is a fifth of a page and is also one record,
  because a reader searching "DokChess" wants that entry, not the top of a list
  it happens to sit in. See the wild loop below.

  Body text is indexed, not just titles. On this site that is the whole point:
  a reader searches for "Drools", "template method" or "quality scenario", and
  those words live in section 8 of an example, never in a page title. That is
  also why this site uses lunr for the masthead suggestions where arc42.de
  uses a lighter title-only scorer — its titles are meaningful on their own,
  and these are not.

  FIELDS
    type     "page", "example" or "wild". The masthead panel groups on it and
             renders the groups in that order (assets/js/search.js: GROUPS).
             A record with a type nothing renders is a record nobody sees.
    system   Which example a section belongs to.
    section  Its number, and null on a system's landing page — the landing page
             is not section 50, that is just where `order` sorts it.
    content  What gets matched. Empty is legal (see `search_body` below).

  `system` and `section` let a hit say WHERE it is: "MaMa-CRM · 3. Context and
  Scope" rather than a bare "Context and Scope", which is the same title in
  every example.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign landings = site.systems | where_exp: 'd', 'd.tagline' -%}
[
{%- assign sep = '' -%}

{%- for doc in site.systems -%}
  {%- assign slug = doc.path | remove_first: '_systems/' | split: '/' | first -%}
  {%- assign sys_url = '/systems/' | append: slug | append: '/' -%}
  {%- assign sys = landings | where: 'url', sys_url | first -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    No system landing page means the directory is a template or is broken; it
    is not something a reader can be sent to.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    A LANDING record carries the tile's front matter as well as its body
    (2026-08-12). The dashboard filter used to match tagline, domain, goal,
    decisions and technologies through a hidden `data-haystack`; the filter is
    gone — this index reaches strictly deeper, and at six examples the eye
    narrows faster than typing — so the fields it matched live here now, or
    "Drools" stops finding MaMa-CRM by search too. `keywords` is the optional
    loose axis alongside them: what the documentation demonstrates, in the
    docs.arc42.org tip vocabulary where it fits (_systems/_TEMPLATE/index.md).
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if sys and doc.title -%}
{{ sep }}{
  "url": {{ doc.url | relative_url | jsonify }},
  "type": "example",
  "title": {{ doc.title | jsonify }},
  "system": {{ sys.title | jsonify }},
  "section": {%- if doc.url == sys_url %} null{% else %} {{ doc.order | jsonify }}{% endif %},
  "content": {% if doc.url == sys_url -%}
    {%- capture landing_matter -%}
      {{ doc.tagline }} {{ doc.domain }} {{ doc.main_goal }}
      {{ doc.decisions | join: ' ' }} {{ doc.technologies | join: ' ' }}
      {{ doc.keywords | join: ' ' }} {{ doc.content | strip_html }}
    {%- endcapture -%}
    {{- landing_matter | normalize_whitespace | strip | jsonify }}
  {%- else %}{{ doc.content | strip_html | strip_newlines | jsonify }}{% endif %}
}
    {%- assign sep = ',' -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}

{%- for page in site.pages -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Real pages only: search.json itself, 404, robots.txt and the sitemap carry
    either no title or nothing a reader wants to land on.

    `search_body: false` indexes the TITLE and nothing else. It exists for a
    page whose body is already indexed record by record somewhere below — index
    it twice and every query matching any one entry also returns the whole
    page, ranked on a body several times longer than any single entry's. The
    title still has to be here or the page becomes unreachable by its own name.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if page.title and page.url != '/404.html' and page.search != false -%}
{{ sep }}{
  "url": {{ page.url | relative_url | jsonify }},
  "type": "page",
  "title": {{ page.title | jsonify }},
  "system": null,
  "section": null,
  "content": {% if page.search_body == false %}""{% else %}{{ page.content | strip_html | strip_newlines | jsonify }}{% endif %}
}
    {%- assign sep = ',' -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}

{%- comment -%}
  /in-the-wild/, one record per entry rather than one for the page.

  The anchor is `entry.title | slugify`, which is what _includes/in-the-wild-
  entry.html puts on the <li>. THE TWO MUST AGREE and nothing checks that they
  do: htmlproofer reads HTML, and this file is JSON, so a change to the anchor
  scheme there breaks every link here silently. Change both or neither.

  Everything a contributor wrote about an entry goes into `content`, including
  our note — the note is where the words a reader actually searches for live
  ("Structurizr", "decision records", "Mermaid"). The host is appended because
  "dokchess" is a plausible query and appears in no other field.

  THE NOTE IS MARKDOWN since 2026-08-15, so it is rendered and then stripped
  rather than indexed as written. Indexed raw, a note containing
  `[openCode](https://opencode.de/en)` would match a search for "https" and for
  "opencode.de", and would show both inside the result excerpt. markdownify
  turns it into HTML and strip_html takes the markup back off, which leaves the
  words a reader can see on the page and nothing else. Do the same to any field
  that becomes markdown later.

  `links` is indexed by LABEL AND HOST, for the same reason the title's host is
  appended above: "urbo.digital" and "swcode.io" are plausible queries, and
  after the strip they appear nowhere else in the record.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- for entry in site.data['in-the-wild'] -%}
  {%- assign host = entry.url | split: '//' | last | split: '/' | first | remove_first: 'www.' -%}
  {%- assign anchor = entry.title | slugify -%}
  {%- capture wild_url -%}/in-the-wild/#{{ anchor }}{%- endcapture -%}
  {%- capture wild_content -%}
    {{ entry.author }} {{ entry.description }} {{ entry.note | markdownify | strip_html }}
    {{ entry.kind }} {{ entry.facets | join: ' ' }} Sections {{ entry.sections }}
    {{ entry.licence }} {{ entry.language | default: 'English' }} {{ entry.year }} {{ host }}
    {%- for link in entry.links -%}
      {{ link.label }} {{ link.url | split: '//' | last | split: '/' | first | remove_first: 'www.' }}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endcapture -%}
{{ sep }}{
  "url": {{ wild_url | relative_url | jsonify }},
  "type": "wild",
  "title": {{ entry.title | jsonify }},
  "system": null,
  "section": null,
  "content": {{ wild_content | normalize_whitespace | strip | jsonify }}
}
  {%- assign sep = ',' -%}
{%- endfor -%}
]
